table.data_collection_procedures:
We collaborate with Pôle emploi, the french Public Employment Service. Jobseekers registered at Pôle emploi are distributed in 5 categories termed from A to E :
A.	Unemployed person, required to realize positive acts of job search (defined as a regular job search, for which the job seeker must be able to produce proofs). The person can look for a job regardless of the type of contract (open-ended or fixed-ended; full time or part time; temporary or seasonal);
B.	A person who has worked a maximum of 78 hours per month, also required to perform positive job search behaviors;
C.	A person who has worked at least 78 hours per month, also required to perform positive job search behaviors;
D.	A person who is unemployed but not immediately available for work and who doesn’t have to perform positive behaviors of job search (because of being in training, sick…);
E.	A person who has a job and isn’t required to perform positive acts of job search. The person can still be registered, because he or she still receives unemployment benefits as a salary complement, to access job trainings…

A random sample of 30 000 jobseekers will be extracted from Pôle emploi’s lists (justification for this number follows below) with respect to a few conditions:
•	The person has to be registered in category A, B or C. We exclude categories D and E because the person doesn’t have to perform job search behaviors (one of our outcome variable);
•	The person is linked to a confirmed email in the information system;
•	The person agreed to receive communications by emails;
•	The person agreed to receive surveys;
•	The person is 18 or over;
We will send jobseekers an email to ask them to participate to a study designed to better know what they think and better tailor Pôle emploi’s services. They will not get paid for this. Participation will clearly be stated as voluntary and anonymous, without any possible consequence for them or their unemployment benefits.

An initial email will be sent to jobseekers in early June 2023. A callback will be sent 2 days later to those who have not responded.
A second follow-up email will be sent at the end of June 2023 to ensure a one-month delay between the two responses.

table.sample_size:
We will extract a random sample of unemployed people:
•	30 000 email addresses will be extracted;
•	Based on Pôle emploi’s expertise, we anticipate 
	o	A response rate of 15% at T0, which means 30 000 * 0.15 = 4 500 complete answers at T0;
	o	A response rate of 25% at T1, which means 4 500 * 0.25 = 1 125 complete answers at T1;

table.manipulated_variables:
We do not manipulate any variable. Again, this study is an observational one with 2 measurement times:
•	Covariates will only be measured at T0;
•	Outcomes will be measured at T0 and T1, except for job search success outcomes which will be measured after a few months (length is unknown at the time of writing);
•	Predictors and scales will only be measured at T0 except for the Job-Search Self-Efficacy Scale which will also be measured at T1 to assess its temporal stability;
Covariates, outcomes and predictors are detailed below.

table.measured_variables:
1) Outcome measures

Confirmatory outcomes are:
- Jobseekers’s behavior :
•	Number of applications (initiated by the jobseeker);
•	Number of clicks on job offers on Pôle emploi’s job posting platform;
•	Time spent on Pôle emploi’s job posting platform;
- Job search outcomes
•	Job search success, as a precarious employment (temporary work and fixed-term contract of less than 6 months;
•	Job search success, as a stable employment (permanent contract)
•	Company creation;

b) Exploratory outcomes are
·        Number of applications (at the initiative of the counselor);
·        Job proposal (at the initiative of a recruiter);

Our rationale is that the confirmatory outcomes depend, at least in part, on the jobseeker's behavior. Our exploratory results depend, at least in part, on external behaviors (behaviors of counselors or recruiters) and may reflect socially (de)valued self-presentation and/or self-fulfilling prophecies.

2) Covariables
Socio-demographic and covariates will be measured via Pôle emploi’s information system, to reduce questionnaire’s length:
·       Age;
·       Gender (Male/Female);
·       Level of education, following the French training nomenclature (Lower than French Baccalauréat (Equivalent to British A-Level), without any diploma (level 6) / Lower than French Baccalauréat, with some diploma (level 5) / Lower than French Baccalauréat, with some (other) diploma (level 5 bis) / French Baccalauréat and similar (level 4) / Up to 2 years of  post-secondary education (level 3) / 3 years of post-secondary education and above (level 2 and 1));
·       Circumstances in which last job ended (Layoff / Contract breach / Resignation / End of fixed-term contract);
·       Area of residence (QPV / Not QPV. "QPV" stand for Quartier prioritaires de la Politique de la Ville which might be translated as “Prioritized neighborhoods of the City Policy”. It refers to a French government urban policy scheme targeting the poorest urban areas which require more intense government intervention);
·       Administrative category of the jobseeker (A / B / C);
·       Length of registration at Pôle emploi in days;
·       Total number of registrations at Pôle Emploi;
·       Number of unemployment benefits days remaining;
·       Number of meetings with the jobseeker’s counselor in the last 3 months;
·       Number of rejections the person has had following an application, in the last 3 months;
·       Type of Pôle Emploi’s support (Follow-up / Guided / Reinforced / Global. Pôle emploi accompanies jobseekers according to four different modalities. Suivi or follow-up is targeted as the most autonomous ones, closest to the labour market. Guidé or guided is targeted at those who need regular support in their search. Renforcé or reinforced is targeted at those who are the furthest from employment and need regular physical meetings with their counselor. Global goes even further, by setting up a pair of advisors from Pôle emploi and local councils or “conseils départementaux” to coordinate social and professionals actions);
·        Number of different target jobs declared;
·       Years spent working in the sector of interest;
·       The job seeker is looking for a full-time job (Yes / No):
·       The job seeker declared having a non-advanced level of French (Yes / No);
·       Wants to become an entrepreneur (Yes / No);

Other covariates will be extracted. At the time of writing, we do not know the categories of these covariates:
·       Type of employment area;
·       Familial situation of the jobseeker;
·       Level of qualification of the individual for the targeted job;
·       Target job sector;
·       Target type of contract;

3) Predictors and scales
Scales’ order is randomized. Order of the items within each scale is also randomized.

a. Job-Search Self-Efficacy (Saks et al., 2015). We translated in French the scale validated by Saks et al. (2015) following the translate/back-translate method suggested by Chapman &amp; Carter (1979) and more recently completed by Cha et al. (2007) resulting in 21 statement after adding a supplementary one that felt missing by the consulted insertion counselor. 
The scale ranges from 0 (strongly disagree) to 5 (strongly agree), with a midpoint at 3 (neither agree nor disagree).

b. Rosenberg's general self-esteem scale (1965). Which has been translated in French by Vallieres &amp; Vallerand (1990) consisting of 10 items. 
Scale ranges from 1 (Strongly disagree) to 4 (Strongly agree) with 2 (Disagree) and 3 (Agree).

c. Camus et al. (2017) Motivation to (re)work scale. This scale builds on Self-Determination Theory to better understand unemployed people’s motivation in seeking a new job. More precisely, the scale consists of 5 subscales assessing autonomous motivation, motivation by identified regulation, motivation by external social and material regulation and amotivation. We present the entire scale to our participants in ordrer to preserve its internal coherence but will only be interested in the autonomous motivation and amotivation subscales as only these two should be related to job-search self-efficacy. The scale consistes in 17 items.
Scale ranges from 1 (Strongly disagree) to 5 (Strongly agree).

We will calculate a mean-score for each scale or subscale.

table.statistical_tests:
Analyses are made with R (R Core Team, 2021) and RStudio (RStudio Team, 2021).

1) We will check each scale’s internal validity by computing Cronbach’s alpha and hierarchical omega with bootstrapped robust 95% confidence intervals bca (Dunn et al., 2014; Kelley &amp; Pornprasertmanit, 2016) with the package MBESS (Kelley, 2022).

2) To assess the structure or factor validity of the JSSE : The scale should be a two correlated factors solution, as in the original study. We will test its structure using the package Lavaan (Rosseel, 2012). If our data does not follow a multivariate normality, as assessed with the Quantile-Quantile Plot (QQ-Plot), we will use bootstrapped standard errors.
We will analyze its structure in two steps:
•	A first exploratory factor analysis will be conducted with a sample of 210 randomly selected respondents (following the convention of 10 people per item);
•	A second confirmatory factor analysis will be conducted with the rest of the sample; 

3) To assess convergent validity, we will rely on Pearson correlations to examine the existence of a positive correlation with Rosenberg's general self-esteem scale (1965), a positive correlation with the autonomous motivation subscale of the Motivation to (re)work scale (Camus et al., 2017), a negative correlation with the amotivation subscale of the Motivation to (re)work scale (Camus et al., 2017). 

4) Job-Search Self-Efficacy should predict actual job search behaviors as well as the number of received job offers (Saks et al., 2015).
Each confirmatory  and exploratory  outcome of interest will be regressed at T0 and T1, each time using the two subscales of Job-Search Self-Efficacy and covariates  measured at T0:
•	First, we will regress each of our exploratory and confirmatory outcomes measured at T1 by the two subscales of job-search self-efficacy and our covariates at T0;
•	Then, we will rerun the same analysis but this time regressing our exploratory and confirmatory outcomes measured at T0 by the two subscales of job-search self-efficacy and our covariates at T0;

5) To assess temporal stability or test-re test reliability we will rely on Pearson correlations as the scale will be administered twice. Results at T0 and T1 should be correlated.

